    "body": "## Finding description\n\n*Follow the provided links in numerical order.*\n\nThe current logic of the contract is that when LaunchToken reaches BONDING_SUPPLY on a buy, the contract should automatically deposit 20% of the token into the UniSwapV2 pool and it should also deposit all of the quoteReserve it holds. \n\nHowever, an attacker can permanently prevent a token from graduating with 1 wei direct deposit into the uniswap pair. In uniswapV2 a pair can be created easily by providing the LaunchToken address and quote address.\n\nHow will the attack work:\n\n1. Lets say that a token is near its graduation, the attacker can deploy the pair (token,quote) before the graduation.\n\n2. He can then make a direct deposit of 1 wei into the pair and call sync, this will bump the internal reserves of tokenA (depends on which is the smaller token, in my ex. I will make the quote token token A) to 1 wei.\n\n3. When graduation comes in _graduate we make a call to _createPairAndSwapRemaining, inside that function we try to add liquidity to the pair by calling ` uniV2Router.addLiquidity`, however given the attacker initiated the attack before that, the call will fail, making the whole transaction revert, disabling graduation forever.\n\nHere is why it fails:\n\nLets look inside _addLiquidity which is called directly addLiquidity by  in the UniSwapV2Router:\n```\n   function _addLiquidity(\n        address tokenA,\n        address tokenB,\n        uint amountADesired,\n        uint amountBDesired,\n        uint amountAMin,\n        uint amountBMin\n    ) private returns (uint amountA, uint amountB) {\n        // create the pair if it doesn't exist yet\n        if (IUniswapV2Factory(factory).getPair(tokenA, tokenB) == address(0)) {\n            IUniswapV2Factory(factory).createPair(tokenA, tokenB);\n        }\n        (uint reserveA, uint reserveB) = UniswapV2Library.getReserves(factory, tokenA, tokenB);\n      @>  if (reserveA == 0 && reserveB == 0) {\n            (amountA, amountB) = (amountADesired, amountBDesired);\n        } else {\n           @> uint amountBOptimal = UniswapV2Library.quote(amountADesired, reserveA, reserveB);\n            if (amountBOptimal <= amountBDesired) {\n                require(amountBOptimal >= amountBMin, 'UniswapV2Router: INSUFFICIENT_B_AMOUNT');\n                (amountA, amountB) = (amountADesired, amountBOptimal);\n            } else {\n                uint amountAOptimal = UniswapV2Library.quote(amountBDesired, reserveB, reserveA);\n                assert(amountAOptimal <= amountADesired);\n                require(amountAOptimal >= amountAMin, 'UniswapV2Router: INSUFFICIENT_A_AMOUNT');\n                (amountA, amountB) = (amountAOptimal, amountBDesired);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n```\nGiven that the attacker made the reserveA for example 1 wei we will go into the first else path, where we will call quote, lets look inside quote:\n\n```\n   function quote(uint amountA, uint reserveA, uint reserveB) internal pure returns (uint amountB) {\n        require(amountA > 0, 'UniswapV2Library: INSUFFICIENT_AMOUNT');\n      @>  require(reserveA > 0 && reserveB > 0, 'UniswapV2Library: INSUFFICIENT_LIQUIDITY');\n        amountB = amountA.mul(reserveB) / reserveA;\n    }\n```\n\nGiven that the attacker called sync, reserveA will be 1 wei, making the call revert as reserveB will be 0.\n\nThis is a permanent DoS because LaunchToken cannot be transfered until graduation, they can only be moved by the launchpad and the gte router. There is no way that anybody moves out token into the pair either, if somebody tries to move it in launchpad and set the final recipient as the pair, we still revert as the function _assertValidRecipient makes sure the final recipient is not the pair.\n\n## Impact\nPermanent DoS in a way that the contract is stopped performing its main functionality, given that in the launch of the new contract a lot of tokens will be created as a lot of users will want to arbitrage from the new functionality GTE offers, an attacker can make a massive attack by suppling 1 wei of quote to each token made. Given that this bug will only appear when one of the newly deployed tokens reach their BONDING_SUPPLY, this can be catastrophic, as users will be forced to pull out all of their quote. \n\nI think this is a High as it breaks core contract functionality. The attack is cheap and easy.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nWhile I don't know if this is the best mitigation, I think its a pretty solid one.\n\nAdd this function:\n```\n  function seedVault(LaunchToken token, address quote) internal {\n        token.mint(1000);\n        token.approve(address(uniV2Router), 1000);\n        quote.safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), 1000);\nquote.safeApprove(address(uniV2Router), 1000)\n        // as token addresses can be determenistic this should be a call that if failed doesn't revert the transaction\n        try uniV2Factory.createPair(address(token), quote) {} catch {}\n        uniV2Router.addLiquidity({\n            tokenA: address(token),\n            tokenB: address(quote),\n            amountADesired: 1000,\n            amountBDesired: 1000,\n            amountAMin: 0,\n            amountBMin: 0,\n            to: address(launchpadLPVault),\n            deadline: block.timestamp\n        });\n    }\n```\nAdd this in launch:\n```\n  function launch(string memory name, string memory symbol, string memory mediaURI)\n        external\n        payable\n        nonReentrant\n        returns (address token)\n    {\n        if (msg.value != launchFee) revert BadLaunchFee();\n\n        address quote = address(currentQuoteAsset);\n        IBondingCurveMinimal curve = currentBondingCurve;\n\n        if (quote == address(0)) revert UninitializedQuote();\n        if (address(curve) == address(0)) revert UninitializedCurve();\n\n        token = address(new LaunchToken(name, symbol, mediaURI, gteRouter));\n\n        curve.initializeCurve(token, TOTAL_SUPPLY, BONDING_SUPPLY);\n        distributor.createRewardsPair(token, quote);\n\n        _launches[token] = LaunchData({active: true, curve: curve, quote: quote});\n\n        @> seedVault(LaunchToken(token), quote);\n\n        emit TokenLaunched({\n            dev: msg.sender,\n            token: token,\n            quoteAsset: quote,\n            bondingCurve: curve,\n            timestamp: block.timestamp,\n            eventNonce: LaunchpadEventNonce.inc()\n        });\n\n        LaunchToken(token).mint(TOTAL_SUPPLY);\n    }\n```\nWe should mint and seed the vault with 1000 wei otherwise it will revert, imo this is the easiest mitigation. Even if somebody before that created a pair and funded it with 1 wei the current design will make the creation of such a token revert from the beginning. So owners can call launch again with different parameters.",
